## Problem

GetOpenPullRequestsAsync fetches all open PRs, then issues one additional GraphQL request per PR, sequentially awaited one at a time, to fetch status-check details.

## Evidence

PullRequestReportService.cs lines 44-51:
var rawPullRequests = await _gitHub.GetOpenPullRequestsAsync(owner, cancellationToken).ConfigureAwait(false);
var enrichedPullRequests = new List<GitHubPullRequest>(rawPullRequests.Count);
foreach (var pullRequest in rawPullRequests)
{
    var statusDetails = await _gitHub.GetPullRequestStatusDetailsAsync(pullRequest, cancellationToken).ConfigureAwait(false);
    enrichedPullRequests.Add(pullRequest with { StatusDetails = statusDetails });
}

## Impact

With the configured defaults (RepositoryLimit=1000, PullRequestLimitPerRepository=100 per appsettings.json/README), an owner with 100-200 open PRs across their repos triggers 100-200 sequential GraphQL round-trips with no concurrency, on top of repository-listing pagination calls. At realistic GraphQL latency (150-300ms/call) that adds 15-60+ seconds to a single request -- a likely timeout for any reverse proxy/load balancer, and a fast way to exhaust GitHub's point-based rate limit on one request.

## Fix

Fetch status-check data with bounded concurrency (Task.WhenAll behind a SemaphoreSlim, or Parallel.ForEachAsync), or better: extend OpenPullRequestsQuery to request statusCheckRollup/branchProtectionRule inline on each PR node so status data returns in the same query as the PR list, eliminating the second round-trip entirely.

## Resolution
Replaced the sequential foreach with Parallel.ForEachAsync (MaxDegreeOfParallelism=8) in PullRequestReportService.FetchAndCacheAsync, fetching status details concurrently instead of one round-trip at a time.

## Problem

/health/ready -- used by the documented Coolify deployment as the orchestrator's health path -- reports "ready" purely based on whether a non-empty token string is configured. It never calls GitHub, so it cannot detect a revoked/expired token or a GitHub outage.

## Evidence

Program.cs lines 67-77:
static IResult GetReadiness(IOptionsMonitor<GitHubOptions> options)
{
    if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(options.CurrentValue.Token))
    {
        return Results.Ok(new HealthResponse("ready"));
    }
    return Results.Problem(title: "GitHub token is not configured.", statusCode: StatusCodes.Status503ServiceUnavailable);
}
README.md lines 252-261 (Coolify deployment section) explicitly recommends /health/ready as the orchestrator's Health path.

## Impact

If the configured token is revoked, expired, or GitHub is unreachable, every real request to /api/github/open-pull-requests fails with a 503, while /health/ready keeps reporting 200/"ready" throughout since it never checks reachability. An orchestrator relying on this probe for restart/alerting decisions will never detect the degraded state.

## Fix

Have the readiness check reflect the last known GitHub call outcome (a cached success/failure flag updated by GitHubGraphQlClient), or perform a cheap, short-timeout authenticated GitHub call (e.g. the rateLimit GraphQL field) on a short cache, so a revoked token or GitHub outage flips readiness to unavailable.

## Resolution
Added GitHubApiHealthState singleton, updated by GitHubGraphQlClient on every success/failure; /health/ready now also checks IsHealthy, not just token presence.

## Problem

On any non-success HTTP status from the GitHub GraphQL endpoint, the entire response body is read and logged at Warning level with no length cap or truncation.

## Evidence

GitHubGraphQlClient.cs lines 254-259:
if (!response.IsSuccessStatusCode)
{
    var body = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken).ConfigureAwait(false);
    _logger.LogWarning("GitHub GraphQL query failed with HTTP {StatusCode}: {Body}", (int)response.StatusCode, body);
    throw new GitHubQueryException($"GitHub GraphQL query failed with HTTP {(int)response.StatusCode}.");
}

## Impact

GitHub API failures don't always return small JSON error bodies -- an edge/CDN-level outage or misroute can return a large HTML error page instead. Logging that verbatim, with no cap, at Warning level on every failed call (which per the N+1/refresh findings can happen frequently) risks noisy, oversized log entries and unnecessary log-ingestion cost.

## Fix

Truncate the logged body to a bounded length (e.g. the first 500 characters) before including it in the log message.

## Resolution
Added a Truncate() helper capping the logged GitHub error response body at 500 characters in GitHubGraphQlClient.ExecuteAsync.
